Id: no-machine-trust-store
Pattern: (\bX509Store\b|StoreName\.Root|StoreName\.CertificateAuthority|ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback|DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificateValidator|ServicePointManager\.ServerCertificateValidationCallback|dev-certs|certutil|security add-trusted-cert|update-ca-certificates)
Subject: *.cs *.csproj *.props *.sln *.sh *.ps1 *.yml *.yaml
Except: none
Issue: 45

This plugin talks to a metadata source over HTTPS, so the shortest route to
testing that path against a local endpoint is a certificate the machine trusts,
and the shortest route to that is installing one into the machine's own store.

That is refused in both spellings. Installing a certificate is an administrative
change to a machine somebody else is using, it outlives the test run, and it
weakens that machine for everything else on it. Turning verification off instead
is worse, because it removes the property the test was supposed to be about
while leaving a test that still passes.

Both spellings are in the pattern because they are the same decision. A run that
cannot verify a certificate and continues anyway has already made the choice the
first spelling makes explicitly, and a rule catching only the honest one would
push the work towards the quiet one.

The subject reaches the workflow files as well as the source, because a step
that trusts a certificate before the suite runs leaves no trace in any test.

The replacement is the injected handler: an outbound call made through the
source adapter goes through a handler a test supplies, so no test needs a real
endpoint and no certificate has to be trusted by anything. That is #45's second
condition and its subject does not exist yet.

Nothing is excepted. The subject names the file types a test, a project file or
a workflow step is written in, and a rule file is none of them, so the pattern
does not reach the place it is written down.

What it cannot do is the whole of the risk. It reads tracked text one line at a
time, so a store opened by reflection, a switch set from an environment variable
the runner supplies, or a certificate trusted by a step that shells out to
something this pattern does not name are all invisible to it. It also says
nothing about what the real endpoint presents, which is not a property any test
in this tree can hold.
